{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_115","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed the full research pass and wrote the deliverables: [planning.md](/workspaces/llm-death-thoughts-89b7-codex/planning.md), [REPORT.md](/workspaces/llm-death-thoughts-89b7-codex/REPORT.md), [README.md](/workspaces/llm-death-thoughts-89b7-codex/README.md), and the end-to-end pipeline in [run_study.py](/workspaces/llm-death-thoughts-89b7-codex/src/research_workspace/run_study.py).\n\nThe main result is that the human-model authenticity gap stayed small and did not get larger for bereavement. Human vs model mean authenticity was `4.92 vs 4.75` for bereavement and `4.67 vs 4.54` for other grief; both gaps were statistically inconclusive, and the source-by-domain interaction was near zero. At the same time, the MAGE detector still separated generated text overall (`AUROC = 0.826`), while the blinded LLM judges labeled every passage as human or human-written. That supports the narrower thesis in your prompt: detectable machine-likeness and judged authenticity came apart, and death did not emerge as a uniquely disqualifying topic for LLM writing in this pilot.\n\nKey outputs are in [results/summary_metrics.json](/workspaces/llm-death-thoughts-89b7-codex/results/summary_metrics.json), [results/text_pool.csv](/workspaces/llm-death-thoughts-89b7-codex/results/text_pool.csv), [results/stat_tests.csv](/workspaces/llm-death-thoughts-89b7-codex/results/stat_tests.csv), and the figures in [figures/authenticity_by_domain_source.png](/workspaces/llm-death-thoughts-89b7-codex/figures/authenticity_by_domain_source.png), [figures/detector_vs_authenticity.png](/workspaces/llm-death-thoughts-89b7-codex/figures/detector_vs_authenticity.png), and [figures/style_features_by_source.png](/workspaces/llm-death-thoughts-89b7-codex/figures/style_features_by_source.png). I also validated the cached rerun path and output counts after the final run."}}
